This is pork tongue and pork snout that has been cooked.

6
00:00:20,841 --> 00:00:23,120
Pureed, pumped or poured.

7
00:00:23,121 --> 00:00:25,760
Now, Cold Cuts on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Cold cuts.

9
00:00:38,001 --> 00:00:41,000
They're cold and they're cut.

10
00:00:41,001 --> 00:00:46,880
Whether at the sandwich shop or deli counter, a picnic or sporting event, cold cuts are the

11
00:00:46,881 --> 00:00:48,880
ultimate inconvenience food.

12
00:00:48,881 --> 00:00:54,000
And they've been that way since they began arriving with European immigrants in the 1800s.

13
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:57,000
They've also become extremely profitable.

14
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:00,860
Every year, Americans consume over $2 billion worth.

15
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:05,940
And although dozens of different cold cuts fill our sandwiches, a few stand out.

16
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:09,060
Some have even become a part of the American lexicon.

17
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:11,440
Like this one.

18
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:16,120
Farmland Foods in Wichita, Kansas is full of baloney.

19
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:20,820
Every year, this plant squeezes out 60 million pounds of it.

20
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:24,140
Enough to make 960 million sandwiches.

21
00:01:24,141 --> 00:01:29,660
But what exactly have Americans been putting in their lunchboxes for all these years?

22
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:34,080
The answer rests in these 2,000-pound combo babies.

23
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:41,060
I'm surrounded right here by the meat ingredients that go into a bologna.

24
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:44,860
Right here, we have what they call 50 trim.

25
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:50,500
That meat will be 50% lean, 50% fat, and will go into this particular combo.

26
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,020
Over here, we have the pork trimmings.

27
00:01:53,021 --> 00:02:00,300
They'll take the trimmings from the loin, from the ham, and this particular meat will be 80% lean, 20% fat.

28
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:06,460
The last thing we have, and the more predominant in bologna, is mechanically separated chicken.

29
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:09,140
Okay, we know you're wondering.

30
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:15,160
Mechanically separating chicken is the process of removing the remaining meat from the bones of an already butchered chicken.

31
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:18,680
And it comprises 70% of bologna.

32
00:02:18,681 --> 00:02:25,340
Named after Bologna, Italy, the town famous for mortadella and other classic handmade sausages,

33
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:30,360
American-style bologna is made with a more modern touch.

34
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:35,720
This is the beginning of the process right here where we start taking big pieces of meat

35
00:02:35,721 --> 00:02:38,460
and making them into smaller meat that we can use.

36
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:42,340
They're spreading that meat out, they're inspecting it as it goes down the belt.

37
00:02:42,341 --> 00:02:47,460
It goes underneath this metal detector where if there has to be any metal in the meat,

38
00:02:47,461 --> 00:02:49,540
It's detected and we can take it out there.

39
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:51,900
From there, it goes into the grinder.

40
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:57,640
At the rate of 10,000 pounds an hour, the meat is ground through a circular die.

41
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:04,620
The smaller surface area on the meat will better absorb the goodies that make up the timeless bologna flavor and texture.

42
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:08,400
You'll see some salt. You'll see some sugar.

43
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:10,400
You'll see some cornstarch.

44
00:03:10,401 --> 00:03:14,160
You'll see some bologna seasoning here, which is mostly mustard.

45
00:03:14,161 --> 00:03:18,160
dump in a blender, ooze in some corn syrup,

46
00:03:18,161 --> 00:03:22,160
and the ingredients are turned into a gooey bologna paste.

47
00:03:22,161 --> 00:03:26,160
Once the product is blended, it's put into this hopper.

48
00:03:26,161 --> 00:03:30,160
It's then run through this vacuumizer where all the air is taken out of the product.

49
00:03:30,161 --> 00:03:34,160
Then run through this last color, powder where it's emulsified,

50
00:03:34,161 --> 00:03:38,160
put into this last hopper ready to stop.

51
00:03:38,161 --> 00:03:42,160
The emulsified mixture makes its final push into thermoplastic cellulose casings.

52
00:03:44,160 --> 00:03:49,580
Once the 36-pound logs are spit out of the casing machine, they're clipped and sealed for cooking.

53
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:58,840
The bologna logs are then wheeled to the hothouse and smoked for eight hours until they reach an internal temperature of 165 degrees Fahrenheit.

54
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:02,660
After a chill down in the cooler, it's off to the slices.

55
00:04:02,661 --> 00:04:16,661
At the rate of 920 slices per minute, the bologna is portioned into 16-ounce piles and sent down

56
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:22,176
the line for packaging. But the final bit of work is left for the consumer.

57
00:04:22,177 --> 00:04:23,020
As you know,

58
00:04:23,021 --> 00:04:28,417
this particular product is stuffed in a red casing. This casing is inedible.

59
00:04:28,418 --> 00:04:29,340
It's put into

60
00:04:29,341 --> 00:04:35,500
package this way mainly for show so it looks good in the case but what you would do before you

61
00:04:35,501 --> 00:04:44,620
actually ate it is you would take that casing peel it away from the meat so that all you really are

62
00:04:44,621 --> 00:04:50,700
eating is baloney the main ingredient for the iconic american sandwich is ready for delivery

63
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:55,500
and there's no telling where it might end up and how it will be used

64
00:04:55,501 --> 00:05:00,040
Elvis Presley was a huge sandwich eater.

65
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:03,080
One of his favorites was fried bologna sandwich,

66
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:05,440
which is still very popular down south.

67
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:07,980
You fry the bologna in a pan, and then you put it in the bread,

68
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:09,240
and then you fry the bread, too.

69
00:05:09,620 --> 00:05:10,720
The more butter, the better.

70
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:15,320
Well before Elvis tore into his first fried bologna sandwich,

71
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:19,580
Julius Caesar and his armies were chomping down on another cold-cut classic,

72
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:21,400
salami.

73
00:05:21,401 --> 00:05:28,500
Legend has it the first salamis were made in 700 BC in Salamis,

74
00:05:28,501 --> 00:05:31,460
a small village on the coast of the island of Cyprus.

75
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:35,200
Derived from the Latin word sal, meaning salt,

76
00:05:36,120 --> 00:05:39,840
salami originated as a way to preserve meat during a time without refrigeration.

77
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:43,540
And those who made it were hailed for their expertise.

78
00:05:44,620 --> 00:05:48,300
I think salami, more so than perhaps any other cold cut,

79
00:05:48,301 --> 00:05:56,060
really is an artisan food. Really a craftsmanship that goes into it is amazing.

80
00:05:56,760 --> 00:06:02,660
At the Columbus Salami Company in San Francisco, California, Francois Vecchio creates Genoa

81
00:06:02,661 --> 00:06:08,080
salami with the same passion and recipe as the ancient sausage makers from Genoa, Italy.

82
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:16,421
Here you have a sample of the kind of meat that we received from the Midwest.

83
00:06:16,422 --> 00:06:17,122
The meat

84
00:06:17,123 --> 00:06:19,280
It needs to be red.

85
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:24,720
We even get whole mussels which are completely peeled, as you see.

86
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:30,080
In the salami, you want to respect that meat all the way through

87
00:06:30,081 --> 00:06:37,060
and add to it the little particle of fat that will give you that nutty taste,

88
00:06:37,061 --> 00:06:44,280
that will give you the texture and the proper balance for flavour in between the lean and the fat.

89
00:06:44,281 --> 00:06:50,620
Shoulder mead and fat from the back of a pig are chopped to size and mixed into the proper

90
00:06:50,621 --> 00:06:51,620
secret proportions.

91
00:06:51,621 --> 00:06:59,620
Now here you see the fat coming into the bowl chopper with a little bit of mead.

92
00:06:59,621 --> 00:07:05,120
Meantime the operator has put in the mead the starter culture.

93
00:07:05,121 --> 00:07:12,040
Now he's putting in the salt and the other ingredients, essentially salt, sugar, some

94
00:07:12,041 --> 00:07:20,240
This is a burgundy wine that is part of the formulation.

95
00:07:20,241 --> 00:07:25,040
The process goes on without emulsification

96
00:07:25,041 --> 00:07:30,640
at low speed to respect the texture of the meat.

97
00:07:30,641 --> 00:07:33,240
Once the salami reaches the right consistency,

98
00:07:33,241 --> 00:07:35,740
it's sent to the blender, where it's turned into a paste.

99
00:07:35,741 --> 00:07:39,140
Through the blending effect, the mixing,

100
00:07:39,141 --> 00:07:43,100
The salt, which is in contact with the protein of the meat,

101
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:47,560
will form a glue that will bind the paste together.

102
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:51,040
Also, we'll bring the lid down

103
00:07:51,041 --> 00:07:57,000
so that we can vacuum whatever air is still included in the paste.

104
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:00,500
Removing the air from the paste

105
00:08:00,501 --> 00:08:03,660
keeps any unwanted bacteria from growing in the meat.

106
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:06,420
A critical step for the next phase.

107
00:08:06,421 --> 00:08:12,720
fermentation the process where beneficial microorganisms work to preserve and cure the

108
00:08:12,721 --> 00:08:18,900
meat once in its casing the outside of the salamis get a kickstart into the fermentation process

109
00:08:18,901 --> 00:08:27,400
with a dousing of penicillium when the salami comes through that little tunnel you see that

110
00:08:27,401 --> 00:08:38,520
sprinkling the salami gets coated with the starter culture for the mold it's all wet outside the mold

111
00:08:38,521 --> 00:08:48,680
is necessary in the process once we go into the fermentation here we have a wagon of genoa salami

112
00:08:48,681 --> 00:08:55,880
ready to go to the fermenting room the salami is warmed inside this 75 degree dripping room

113
00:08:55,881 --> 00:09:01,880
The fermentation process is underway, a critical step for the meat to become salami.

114
00:09:01,881 --> 00:09:04,880
Remember that starter culture added to the meat?

115
00:09:04,881 --> 00:09:08,880
Inside the salami, the starter culture starts to multiply.

116
00:09:08,881 --> 00:09:15,880
Billions of microscopic bacteria consume the meat's natural sugar particles, turning them into lactic acid.

117
00:09:15,881 --> 00:09:20,880
The lactic acid coagulates the meat proteins into solids.

118
00:09:20,881 --> 00:09:26,560
As the meat dries, the bacteria begin to die off.

119
00:09:26,561 --> 00:09:31,200
At the same time, the penicillium mold is attacking from the outside.

120
00:09:31,201 --> 00:09:36,400
The mold penetrates the casing, acting as a biological catalyst, releasing flavor and

121
00:09:36,401 --> 00:09:40,280
aromatic enzymes that give the salami its distinctive taste.

122
00:09:40,281 --> 00:09:47,840
Meanwhile, as moisture continues to evaporate, the sausage loses about 5% of its weight.

123
00:09:47,841 --> 00:09:50,280
By the fifth day, it looks like this.

124
00:09:50,281 --> 00:09:53,620
We're getting to the end. Normally it takes seven days to dry.

125
00:09:54,040 --> 00:09:56,920
In this case, the salami is fully formed.

126
00:09:57,560 --> 00:09:59,260
The mold has grown everywhere.

127
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:05,040
And if I cut it, voila, I have a salami.

128
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:08,040
The slice holds.

129
00:10:08,860 --> 00:10:11,120
It's not ready, but it's delicious.

130
00:10:12,200 --> 00:10:14,460
For those who like their meat even firmer,

131
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:16,580
the salami heads for the aging room,

132
00:10:16,920 --> 00:10:18,820
where it will hang for up to three months.

133
00:10:20,280 --> 00:10:21,960
Once it's dried, it's ready to eat.

134
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:26,120
The salami has reached its full flavor potential,

135
00:10:26,560 --> 00:10:29,100
but left alone, it would continue to dry

136
00:10:29,101 --> 00:10:31,300
and lose that Genoa salami taste.

137
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:34,500
So after the mold is peeled off,

138
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:36,040
the salami is sliced

139
00:10:36,041 --> 00:10:40,860
and then sealed.

140
00:10:41,860 --> 00:10:44,300
Columbus Genoa salami is now ready for market.

141
00:10:46,100 --> 00:10:48,960
They even make the most expensive type of salami here.

142
00:10:48,961 --> 00:10:53,740
It's called Foligno, and it runs about $15 a pound.

143
00:10:55,960 --> 00:11:00,960
If you like salami, you might like the classic cold cut that rhymes with it, pastrami.

144
00:11:02,080 --> 00:11:04,780
At the world-famous Carnegie Deli in New York City,

145
00:11:05,420 --> 00:11:09,220
these mouth-watering masterworks have been luring patrons since 1937.

146
00:11:09,940 --> 00:11:14,800
The signature sign that's been with us for 70 years,

147
00:11:14,801 --> 00:11:16,840
this is a landmark, a trademark.

148
00:11:16,841 --> 00:11:18,840
It says the world's best.

149
00:11:18,841 --> 00:11:21,840
We have the world's best pastrami right here in New York.

150
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:27,840
Every week, Carnegie customers devour more than 10,000 pounds of the stuff.

151
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:32,840
To make this product is very, very hard and very time-consuming.

152
00:11:32,841 --> 00:11:35,840
It takes us 14 days from the time until we get the meat,

153
00:11:35,841 --> 00:11:38,840
until we process the meat, until we serve it.

154
00:11:40,840 --> 00:11:43,840
And that starts at the Carnegie Processing Plant in New Jersey,

155
00:11:43,841 --> 00:11:49,560
New Jersey, where every day so-called steer navels arrive to be turned into pastrami.

156
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:56,500
This is where the Carnegie Deli pastrami sandwich starts, right in this room, right over here.

157
00:11:57,200 --> 00:12:01,426
As you can see, these navels are very heavily marbled.

158
00:12:01,427 --> 00:12:04,400
The marbling means that they're either

159
00:12:04,401 --> 00:12:08,856
prime or choice. If we had a lean pastrami, we wouldn't have any flavor.

160
00:12:08,857 --> 00:12:10,280
Fat is where the flavor

161
00:12:10,281 --> 00:12:12,280
as we all know that.

162
00:12:12,281 --> 00:12:16,920
This navel comes from the section from the fourth rib down all the way down past its

163
00:12:16,921 --> 00:12:18,800
belly button.

164
00:12:18,801 --> 00:12:22,900
The navel as you can see almost looks like a piece of bacon that's because we call it

165
00:12:22,901 --> 00:12:23,900
steer bacon.

166
00:12:23,901 --> 00:12:29,740
The navels start their journey in the injection room where 54 needles deliver a dose of brine.

167
00:12:29,741 --> 00:12:33,960
This is called a stitch pump machine and that's really basically what it's doing.

168
00:12:33,961 --> 00:12:37,240
It's stitch pumping through the needles the brine that we make.

169
00:12:37,241 --> 00:12:43,380
The brine contains water, salt, and all the special spices that make our Carnegie Deli pastrami what it is.

170
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:46,300
The brine preserves the meat in the red color.

171
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:50,180
You get a nice rosy red color, the same as they treat bacon.

172
00:12:50,460 --> 00:12:56,000
If you look closely at the navels, you'll see all the needle marks that have gone through the meat.

173
00:12:57,140 --> 00:13:00,800
The brine-filled navels are then sent to recover in a steel tub.

174
00:13:00,801 --> 00:13:06,180
For the next two or three days, they absorb the brine and spices until it's time for a

175
00:13:06,181 --> 00:13:08,680
special Carnegie Deli spice massage.

176
00:13:08,681 --> 00:13:13,720
All right, this is where the magic happens, right here.

177
00:13:13,721 --> 00:13:18,240
We've already drained the navels that you saw in the holding tanks in the other room,

178
00:13:18,241 --> 00:13:20,900
and now we rub them with all our secret spices that we use.

179
00:13:20,901 --> 00:13:26,740
That includes coriander, peppercorn, paprika, and caramel color.

180
00:13:26,741 --> 00:13:29,140
And then there's a few in there I'm just going to keep to myself.

181
00:13:30,700 --> 00:13:34,500
With their spice coat on, the navels are hung and sent to the smokers,

182
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:37,040
where they're cooked with the aroma of hickory wood.

183
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:40,600
Other manufacturers boil their navels.

184
00:13:41,740 --> 00:13:44,840
Four hours later, the navels have graduated to pastrami.

185
00:13:45,020 --> 00:13:46,260
Take a little cut off of here.

186
00:13:48,660 --> 00:13:50,660
Got all the fat. We got all the marbling.

187
00:13:50,661 --> 00:13:53,220
This really looks like a nice prime piece of meat.

188
00:13:53,221 --> 00:13:56,660
Ah, the smell.

189
00:13:56,661 --> 00:13:58,560
We're gonna let it go to Carnegie Deli.

190
00:13:58,561 --> 00:14:04,400
Cooled and crated, the pastrami is off to the deli, where it gets one last pampering

191
00:14:04,401 --> 00:14:07,120
in a nice, long steam bath.

192
00:14:07,121 --> 00:14:11,520
Once the meat comes in, we bring it and we put it into the steam table.

193
00:14:11,521 --> 00:14:15,820
Here in the steam table, we cook it for two to two and a half hours to bring all the flavor

194
00:14:15,821 --> 00:14:20,660
and the moisture out, and it breaks down the fiber, and we get a product nice and soft

195
00:14:20,661 --> 00:14:24,500
and tender, ready to go upstairs, make sandwiches, and to eat it.

196
00:14:25,140 --> 00:14:29,100
Upstairs, people from around the world are digging in on the time-honored recipe

197
00:14:29,101 --> 00:14:32,320
brought over by European Jews in the mid-1800s.

198
00:14:34,820 --> 00:14:36,940
Finishing a sandwich, however, is another thing.

199
00:14:37,380 --> 00:14:40,020
Every sandwich that comes out is at least a pound of meat.

200
00:14:40,860 --> 00:14:42,660
We don't want our customers to finish.

201
00:14:43,040 --> 00:14:45,360
If they finish, we made a mistake.

202
00:14:46,040 --> 00:14:49,360
And if they can open their mouth and put their mouth around it, it's a mistake.

203
00:14:50,660 --> 00:14:54,100
As the tenderized masterpiece makes its final approach,

204
00:14:54,640 --> 00:14:56,280
it's held up with care and tradition,

205
00:14:56,580 --> 00:14:58,940
all the way to another delighted customer.

206
00:15:00,760 --> 00:15:03,580
In 2003, a sausage factory in northern Denmark

207
00:15:03,581 --> 00:15:05,520
created the world's longest salami.

208
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:09,720
If laid end to end, it would stretch nearly 500 feet,

209
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:12,420
surpassing the height of the Statue of Liberty

210
00:15:12,421 --> 00:15:14,660
by almost 200 feet.

211
00:15:16,280 --> 00:15:18,760
Cold Cuts will return on Modern Marvels.

212
00:15:20,660 --> 00:15:25,460
Bologna, salami, and pastrami may be the classic cold cuts known to everyone.

213
00:15:26,180 --> 00:15:29,420
But now it's time for some more challenging compositions.

214
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:33,900
Fritz Eusinger is a fourth-generation sausage maker.

215
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:40,720
In 1880, his great-grandfather, Fred, immigrated to the United States and settled in Milwaukee,

216
00:15:41,020 --> 00:15:43,360
where he opened Eusinger's sausage company.

217
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:50,480
With him came his favorite European cold-cut recipes, including some on the exotic side.

218
00:15:50,660 --> 00:15:51,980
like head cheese.

219
00:15:52,380 --> 00:15:54,860
To the squeamish, a quick heads up.

220
00:15:55,320 --> 00:15:56,640
There's nothing cheesy about it.

221
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:58,740
These are the meats that have been prepared

222
00:15:58,741 --> 00:16:01,080
that are going to go into the batch of head cheese.

223
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:05,920
The ingredients are meats that come from the head of the hog.

224
00:16:06,280 --> 00:16:09,560
That is pork tongue and pork snout that has been cooked,

225
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:12,700
it's been cubed, and then it's going to be mixed with spices.

226
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:17,180
Head cheese has a loyal following among hardcore cold-cut lovers.

227
00:16:17,700 --> 00:16:20,460
One with its roots planted deep in the family farm.

228
00:16:20,660 --> 00:16:26,660
And historically, head cheese was a product that was developed generations ago

229
00:16:26,661 --> 00:16:30,660
when people raised their own animals on the farm to feed their family.

230
00:16:30,661 --> 00:16:36,660
The beauty of head cheese is that it makes use of all of the meats from the animal.

